// Practice quiz: Beans and bean scopes
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-boot-basics/beans-and-bean-scopes

::o1 registering library types::You need a bean of a library class you cannot edit. Which registration fits? {
~Add @Service to the library class#Not quite. You cannot edit its source.
=A @Bean method in a @Configuration class#Correct. You cannot add annotations to a class you do not own.
~Component scanning alone#Not quite. Scanning only finds annotated classes in scanned packages.
~A static field in main#Not quite. That is not registering a bean.
}

::o1 bean naming::What is the default name of a bean from a @Bean method called auditClock? {
~The return type name#Not quite. The return type drives injection by type, not the name.
~The configuration class name#Not quite. That class is a separate bean.
=auditClock#Correct. The method name is the default bean name.
~clock#Not quite. Only the exact method name is used.
}

::o2 singleton::Two constructors each inject the same singleton bean. How many instances exist? {
~Two#Not quite. That is prototype behavior.
~One per request#Not quite. That is request scope.
=One#Correct. Singleton means one instance per container.
~One per thread#Not quite. Spring does not default to thread-based instances.
}

::o2 prototype in singleton::A prototype bean is injected once into a singleton. How often is a new prototype created for that field? {
~On every method call#Not quite. The field keeps the same reference.
~On every request#Not quite. Nothing refreshes the field.
~Never#Not quite. It is created once.
=Once, when the singleton is created#Correct. Injection happens once at construction.
}

::o3 stateless services::Where should a singleton service keep the current request data? {
=In local variables and method parameters#Correct. Each call has its own stack, so concurrent requests do not collide.
~In an instance field#Not quite. A field on a shared bean is shared across requests.
~In a static field#Not quite. Static is shared even more widely.
~In a private final field#Not quite. Final stops reassignment, but the value would still be shared.
}

